Q: How to check if a function is a constructor A: <p>If speed is what you're after and you're not worried about edge cases:</p>
<pre><code>typeof value=='function' &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value
</code></pre>
<p>But these days there are better ways, like this cross-browser solution that also works with bound functions. Works in both modern and ES3+ browsers / nodejs:</p>
<pre><code>var isConstructor = typeof Proxy=='function'?
function(value) {// Works in modern environments that support Proxy (ES6+)
    if (typeof value=='function') {
        try {// Throws if `value` is not a constructor function
            var success = new new Proxy(value, {construct:Object});
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return !!success
}:
function(value) {// Tested in pre-ES6 JS including Internet Explorer 6 and up
    // Object.prototype.toString() is better than `typeof` in pre-ES6 JS
    // since it rules out generators and typeof returns unexpected results
    var is_function = {}.toString.call(value)=='[object Function]';

    // If prototype property is in function, it's probably a constructor
    // except IE where !!alert.prototype//true for host functions
    var has_proto_prop = is_function &amp;&amp; 'prototype' in value;

    // If function has a prototype property and is not a built-in
    // function starting with lowercase, it is probably a construtor
    var success = has_proto_prop &amp;&amp; !/^\s*\w+ [a-z_]\w*\(\) {\s*\[native c/.test(value);

    // In IE, value could be a constructor where typeof value=='object'
    // Or no prototype property means value might be a bound constructor
    if (typeof value=='object' || is_function &amp;&amp; !has_proto_prop) {
        try {
            // instanceof throws when no prototype property in bound target
            // Doesn't throw if value is constructor where typeof value=='object'
            success = {} instanceof value || true
        }
        catch(e) {
        }
    }
    return success
}
isConstructor(window)//false
isConstructor(alert)//false
isConstructor(XMLHttpRequest)//true
isConstructor(Array.bind())//true
isConstructor(function myFunc(){})//true
isConstructor(function* myGenerator(){})//false
// Caveat: IE returns false-positives on bound host objects
isConstructor(isNaN.bind())//false (works in IE since isNaN isn't a host object)
isConstructor(alert.bind())//false (however in IE this returns true (false-positive))
</code></pre>
<p>Here's best of both worlds. This prioritizes speed over edge cases while still supporting bound functions in ES6+ environments:</p>
<pre><code>function isConstructorFast(value) {
    if (typeof value=='function') {
        if (value.prototype) {
            var success = true;
        }
        else if (/^bound /.test(value.name)) {
            try {
                success = new new Proxy(value, {construct:Object});
            }
            catch(e) {
            }
        }
    }
    return !!success;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to programmatically scroll to a specific cell in nested ScrollViews (vertical containing horizontal)? A: <p>Just to recap on some key constraints:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>The top level <code>ScrollView</code> includes some header content as well as the grid of cells. All of this content should scroll together when scrolled vertically.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The nested grid should also be able to scroll horizontally. The whole grid should scroll as one view, the rows of the grid should not scroll independently. The important thing is that horizontal scrolling should not impact the header region, which should remain in the same position.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>In your point 3, you described the problem when the outer <code>ScrollView</code> is wrapped with a <code>ScrollViewReader</code></p>
<blockquote>
<ol start="3">
<li><strong><code>ScrollViewReader</code> on vertical <code>ScrollView</code></strong> — The vertical proxy cannot find IDs that are inside the nested horizontal ScrollView.</li>
</ol>
</blockquote>
<p>A workaround for this problem would be to use placeholders in the background of the grid. Something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>gridView
    .background {
        VStack(spacing: 0) {
            ForEach(myMap[&quot;A&quot;] ?? [], id: \.self) { row in
                Color.clear
                    .id(row)
            }
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>This elevates the targets for scrolling to a view that is outside of the nested (horizontal) <code>ScrollView</code>.</p>
<p>Using simple <code>Color.clear</code> as placeholder only works if all the rows of the grid have the same height. If this is not the case, you might need to use dummy content which is then hidden, for example:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>ForEach(myMap[&quot;A&quot;] ?? [], id: \.self) { row in
    CellContent(col: &quot;A&quot;, row: row)
        .disabled(true)
        .hidden()
        .accessibilityHidden(true)
        .id(row)
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>To perform the actual programmatic scrolling in a way compatible with iOS 16, you could consider passing the <code>ScrollViewProxy</code> to nested views. Alternatively, you can use a state variable to hold the id to scroll to. Then, use either <code>.onChange</code> or <code>.task</code> to detect changes to the state variable from a view where the proxy is already in scope. This is effectively a home-made <code>.scrollPosition</code>.</p>
<p>If a state variable is used, it only works if the variable actually <em>changes</em>. So consider the case of where a programmatic scroll is performed, then a manual scroll, then a programmatic scroll to the same target again. In this case, the target will not have changed, so no scroll is performed. To solve this, it is a good idea to reset the previous target first, then update the state variable to the target asynchronously.</p>
<hr />
<p>Here is how the example can be updated to use the techniques described above. Some of the containers have also been changed to lazy containers, just to make sure that it can work when this is the case too.</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct CellKey {
    let col: String
    let row: Int
}

struct NestedScrollingView: View {
    @StateObject var viewModel = NestedScrollingViewModel()
    let myMap = Dictionary(uniqueKeysWithValues: [&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;, &quot;D&quot;, &quot;E&quot;, &quot;F&quot;, &quot;G&quot;, &quot;H&quot;, &quot;I&quot;, &quot;J&quot;, &quot;K&quot;].map { ($0, Array(1...100)) })
    @State private var targetKey: CellKey?

    private func scrollTo(key: CellKey) {
        if targetKey == nil {
            targetKey = key
        } else {
            targetKey = nil
            Task { @MainActor in
                targetKey = key
            }
        }
    }

    var body: some View {
        ScrollViewReader { rowProxy in
            ScrollView(.vertical, showsIndicators: false) {
                LazyVStack {
                    RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 8)
                        .foregroundStyle(Color.blue)
                        .frame(height: 100)
                        .padding()
                    Button(&quot;Target Scroll View \(viewModel.scrollTokey.description)&quot;) {
                        // viewModel.onClickScrollTo()
                        scrollTo(key: CellKey(col: &quot;K&quot;, row: 60))
                    }
                    .buttonStyle(.borderedProminent)
                    .padding()

                    gridView
                        .background {
                            VStack(spacing: 0) {
                                ForEach(myMap[&quot;A&quot;] ?? [], id: \.self) { row in
                                    Color.clear
                                        .id(row)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                }
                .task(id: targetKey?.row) {
                    if let row = targetKey?.row {
                        withAnimation {
                            rowProxy.scrollTo(row)
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    var gridView: some View {
        ScrollViewReader { colProxy in
            ScrollView(.horizontal, showsIndicators: false) {
                LazyHStack(alignment: .top, spacing: 4) {
                    ForEach(myMap.keys.sorted(), id: \.self) { key in
                        VStack(spacing: 4) {
                            Text(key)
                                .font(.caption)

                            ForEach(myMap[key] ?? [], id: \.self) { value in
                                let isSelected = targetKey?.col == key &amp;&amp; targetKey?.row == value
                                Text(&quot;\(key):\(value)&quot;)
                                    .font(.caption)
                                    .frame(width: 50, height: 50)
                                    .border(Color.gray, width: 1)
                                    .background(isSelected ? .yellow : Color(.systemBackground))
                                    .onTapGesture { scrollTo(key: CellKey(col: key, row: value)) }
                                    .padding(4)
                            }
                        }
                        .id(key)
                    }
                }
                .task(id: targetKey?.col) {
                    if let col = targetKey?.col {
                        withAnimation {
                            colProxy.scrollTo(col)
                        }
                    }
                }
                .padding(8)
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
