Q: How to print useful error messages with `_Generic` in C A: <blockquote>
<p>Printing error messages should be but simple.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When code has self-detected an exit-able problem, assume the worst.  Do nothing complicate.  I'd recommend no fancy function calls (e.g. use fputs()<code>rather than</code>fprintf()`.  Consider the program may die before the error message is fully form and printed.</p>
<pre><code>// Example
void on_error(int code, const char *something) {
  fputc('\n', stderr);
  fputs(something, stderr);
  fputc('\n', stderr);
  exit(code);
}
</code></pre>

Q: fast-csv append writing headers to last row A: <p>The issue in your code comes down to how <strong>fast-csv</strong> handles headers when appending. In your snippet, you’re passing <code>{ headers: false }</code> to <code>csvStream.write()</code>, but the library actually expects the option to be called <code>writeHeaders</code>, not <code>headers</code>.</p>
<p>So when you append, fast-csv still thinks it should write headers again, which is why you’re seeing them show up with the last item.</p>
<p>Here’s how you can fix it:</p>
<p>js</p>
<pre><code>const csv = require('fast-csv');
const fs = require('fs');
const config = require('./config.json');

const date = new Date().getFullYear();
const filename = `${date}${config.filename}`;

async function createOrAppend(data) {
  const fileExists = fs.existsSync(filename);
  const writeStream = fs.createWriteStream(filename, { flags: fileExists ? 'a' : 'w' });
  const csvStream = csv.format({ headers: !fileExists, writeHeaders: !fileExists });

  csvStream.pipe(writeStream);

  data.forEach(item =&gt; {
    csvStream.write(item);
  });

  csvStream.end();

  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
    writeStream.on('finish', resolve);
    writeStream.on('error', reject);
  });
}

module.exports = { createOrAppend };
</code></pre>
